The maintenability can be defined as "a characteristic of design and installation expressed as a probability that an item will be retained in or restored to specified conditionswithin a given period of time, when maintenance action is performed in accordance with prescribed procedures and resources." (AMCP 706-133, 1976). In order to keep products performances and values for long time, products should be able to be maintained properly. Customers or experts should easily, quickly and cheaply be able to access, repair or upgrade the product. This will avoid the dissatisfaction of the user with the product and technical obsolescence. Companies can work on data informations to predict the interval of time of the technical failure and prevent it through a use-based maintenance. Improving the effectiveness of maintenance,  particularly for PSS, reduce costs related to maintenance actions and, in the same time, increase job  growth. 

Design for maintainability means also:
•  Design a program to maintain products regularly
•  Design a product easy to maintain
•  Provide a possible kit or a manual to help users in maintenance their product
•  Design for a technical and interactive simplicity
•  Design for a multi-systemic application 
•  Design the product for a better prediction of maintenance 
•  Design for proactive maintenance ( such as regular cleaning or lubrification)